<!-- ─────────────── §01 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-why" data-code="why">
<div class="section-no">01 — Why GPU% Isn't Enough</div>
<h2>The Problem with nvidia-smi</h2>
<p>When your training run shows "GPU utilization: 85%" in <code>nvidia-smi</code>, what does that actually mean? It means the GPU shader processors were <em>scheduled</em> for 85% of clock cycles — not that they were doing useful model computation. A GPU stuck in a data-loading stall, or waiting for activations to be read from HBM, can still report high "utilization."</p>
<p>Two runs can both show 80% GPU utilization while one is 5× more efficient than the other. The canvas below shows this concretely: two training dashboards with near-identical GPU% but wildly different actual MFU.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">utilization-canvas — same GPU%, different MFU</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">The gap is real</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="util-canvas" style="width:100%;height:210px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>MFU cuts through this ambiguity. It measures: of the GPU's <em>theoretical peak throughput</em>, how much is landing on actual weight × activation multiply-adds in your model? That's the number worth tracking.</p>

<h3>The Definition</h3>
<p>MFU was popularized by Google's PaLM paper (2022) as a clean efficiency ratio:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Model FLOP Utilization</div>
  <span class="math-var">MFU</span> = <span class="math-var">Observed FLOPs/s</span> / <span class="math-var">Peak Hardware FLOPs/s</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">// = (tokens/s × FLOPs per token) / (num_GPUs × GPU peak FLOPs/s)</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">// MFU = 1.0 means you're using every last FLOP of GPU capacity — theoretical max</span>
</div>
<p>In practice, top training stacks reach 40–50% MFU. Below 20% suggests a serious bottleneck: data loading, excessive communication, or a memory-bandwidth-bound configuration.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §02 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-flops" data-code="flops">
<div class="section-no">02 — The 6N Rule</div>
<h2>How Many FLOPs Does One Token Cost?</h2>
<p>To compute MFU, you need <strong>FLOPs per token</strong> — how many floating-point operations your model performs for each new token. For a transformer with <em>N</em> parameters, a remarkably clean approximation holds:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">FLOPs per token (training)</div>
  FLOPs<sub>token</sub> ≈ <span class="math-var">6N</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">// where N = total model parameters</span>
  <br/><br/>
  Derivation: each param participates in one multiply-add per token
  <br/>
  Forward pass: <span class="math-var">2N</span> FLOPs  (1 multiply + 1 add per param ≈ 2 FLOPs)
  <br/>
  Backward pass: <span class="math-var">4N</span> FLOPs  (gradient for weights + gradient for activations)
  <br/>
  Total: <span class="math-var">2N + 4N = 6N</span>
</div>

<p>This 6N approximation ignores attention's quadratic cost (O(T²·d) in sequence length T), which is accurate for short contexts but increasingly wrong for long ones. The chart below shows how the FLOPs breakdown shifts as sequence length grows — attention becomes dominant past ~8K tokens for a 7B model.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">flops-breakdown — Llama 2 7B forward-pass FLOPs by component</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Attention grows quadratically with context length</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="flops-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>For most training at ≤ 4K context, the 6N rule is accurate within 20%. For 32K+ contexts (like long-document fine-tuning), you should use the full formula below to avoid underestimating FLOPs — and therefore <em>overestimating</em> MFU:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Full FLOPs per token (training, including attention)</div>
  FLOPs<sub>token</sub> = 6N + 12 · <span class="math-var">L</span> · <span class="math-var">T</span> · <span class="math-var">d</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">// L = number of transformer layers</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">// T = sequence length (context window)</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">// d = model hidden dimension</span>
  <br/>
  <span class="math-comment">// For Llama 2 7B: 6×6.7B + 12×32×2048×4096 = 40.2B + 3.2B ≈ 43.4B</span>
</div>

<h3>Concrete Numbers for Common Models</h3>
<table class="results-table">
