<section id="s-walkthrough" data-code="walkthrough">
<div class="section-no">04 — Llama 2 7B Walk-Through</div>
<h2>Computing MFU Step by Step</h2>
<p>Let's compute MFU from scratch for Meta's actual Llama 2 7B training run — no black boxes. Meta released the compute budget in their paper: <strong>184,320 GPU-hours on 2048 A100s to process 2 trillion tokens</strong>. Walk through the four steps below:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">mfu-steps — Llama 2 7B on A100 80GB SXM4</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Use Prev / Next to step through</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="wt-steps">
      <div class="step-box" id="wt-s0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Count model parameters (N)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Llama 2 7B has 6.74 billion parameters across 32 transformer layers (d=4096, 32 heads, FFN width 11,008). Vocabulary embedding and un-embedding layers add ~1B — we typically count these too since they participate in every token's forward pass.</div>
          <div class="step-code">N = 6.74 × 10⁹ ≈ 6.7B params</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="wt-s1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Estimate FLOPs per token using 6N</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Training = forward (2N) + backward (4N) = 6N per token. This excludes attention FLOPs (small at T=2048: +3.2B, about 7%), so 6N is a conservative estimate that slightly under-counts real work.</div>
          <div class="step-code">FLOPs/token = 6 × 6.7B = 40.2 × 10⁹ = 40.2 GFLOPs</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="wt-s2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Measure observed throughput</div>
          <div class="step-desc">From Meta's numbers: 184,320 GPU-hours × 3,600 s/hr = 663.6M GPU-seconds. Total tokens = 2T. Throughput = 2T / 663.6M ≈ 3,014 tokens/GPU/s. For 2048 GPUs in parallel: global = 3,014 × 2048 ≈ 6.17M tokens/s.</div>
          <div class="step-code">T = 2T tokens / 663.6M GPU-s = 3,014 tok/GPU/s</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="wt-s3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Divide by peak hardware throughput</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A100 SXM4 peak BF16 = 312 TFLOPs/s. Actual FLOPs/GPU/s = 3,014 × 40.2G = 121.2 TFLOPs/s. MFU = 121.2 / 312 = <strong>38.8%</strong> — squarely in the "good" range for production training.</div>
          <div class="step-code">MFU = 121.2 TFLOPs / 312 TFLOPs = 38.8%</div>
          <div class="step-result">&#10003; MFU = 38.8% — consistent with Meta's reported efficiency</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="wt-prev" disabled>&#8592; Prev</button>
      <button id="wt-next">Next &#8594;</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="wt-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Cross-check: Compute Budget Consistency</h3>
<p>You can verify this a different way: Chinchilla-optimal for 6.7B params suggests 134B training tokens (20× params). Llama 2 uses 2T — about 15× more, intentionally compute-over-trained for inference efficiency. That's reflected in the lower-than-optimal MFU (well-utilized hardware but more passes over the data than the "optimal" Chinchilla budget).</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────── §05 ─────────────── -->
<section id="s-overhead" data-code="overhead">
<div class="section-no">05 — What Steals Your MFU?</div>
<h2>The MFU Budget: Where Does Compute Go?</h2>
<p>If the theoretical max is 100% and typical runs hit 35–50%, where does the rest go? The four main culprits:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">overhead-canvas — MFU efficiency budget breakdown</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">A good run at ~40% MFU</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="overhead-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Communication overhead (~15–25%)</strong> — AllReduce for gradient synchronization across GPUs eats bandwidth. NVLink (A100–A100: 600 GB/s) helps but pipeline stalls still occur with pipeline parallelism. Large tensor-parallel groups increase communication volume quadratically.</li>
  <li><strong>Memory bandwidth bottleneck (~10–20%)</strong> — Attention's KV cache, optimizer states (Adam = 12 bytes/param), and gradient accumulation buffers all live in HBM. When the compute-to-memory-access ratio is too low (small batch, short sequence), the GPU stalls waiting for data — not running compute.</li>
  <li><strong>Kernel launch overhead (~5–10%)</strong> — Each PyTorch op dispatches a CUDA kernel. With gradient checkpointing, the number of kernels doubles. Fusing ops (FlashAttention, fused layer norms) reduces this significantly.</li>
  <li><strong>Data loading &amp; pipeline bubbles (~3–8%)</strong> — CPU-side tokenization and batching, pipeline parallel bubbles (the "startup tax" at beginning and end of each micro-batch pipeline). Larger micro-batches amortize the bubble cost.</li>
</ul>

<h3>MFU vs HFU</h3>
<p>There's a related metric: <strong>Hardware FLOP Utilization (HFU)</strong>. The difference is whether you count gradient checkpointing recomputation:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>MFU</strong> counts only the "useful" model FLOPs (6N per token) — no recompute.</li>
  <li><strong>HFU</strong> adds the recompute cost. With selective activation recompute (~1/3 of layers), FLOPs increase by ~1.33×, so HFU ≈ MFU × 1.33.</li>
</ul>
<p>PaLM reports "hardware FLOP utilization" of 46.2% using the 6N counting — which is effectively MFU by today's terminology. When comparing numbers across papers, always check which definition they use.</p>
</section>

